    textEn: 'I will put enmities between thee and the woman, and thy seed and her seed: she shall crush thy head, and thou shalt lie in wait for her heel.',
    fullSourceEn: 'Book of Genesis, chapter 3, verse 15 (the Protoevangelium)',
    meaningEn: 'Feminine noun: enmity, hostility, deep antagonism. It is not a mere feeling but lasting hostility, the same word Ezekiel uses for the age-old hatred of nations hostile to Israel (Ez 25:15 and 35:5). The Portuguese Ave Maria Bible renders it "ódio" (hatred), while the Douay-Rheims has "enmities", following the Latin Vulgate (inimicitias). All translate the same Hebrew word. The argument of "total enmity" (or "absolute enmity") is theological: if God places this radical opposition between the woman and the serpent (representing sin and the devil), Mary cannot at any moment be under the dominion of the serpent, that is, under original sin. This total separation between Mary and sin is one of the Old Testament roots of the dogma of the Immaculate Conception. Pius IX (Ineffabilis Deus, 1854) and Pius XII (Fulgens Corona, 1953, §7) read this verse in that sense.',
